{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1793917842,"nonce":"Np/5/KVkXSOR3xmj4byAWQeqIQk=","target":"e66709b09b254d1ba7edeb58254eda214d806d35b8d6c129aeae303d48ae316e","message":"**Problem.** The tracker's remote-tracking refs live at `refs/remotes/\u003cremote\u003e/work-*/\u003cid\u003e`. Under the default refspec `+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/\u003cremote\u003e/*`, git reads them as branches. `git remote prune` and `git fetch --prune` map them back to `refs/heads/work-*`, find nothing there, and delete them all. The next `git work pull` fetches everything again, so a branch-sync script that prunes and `git work sync` keep undoing each other. Nothing is lost, but every run re-fetches the whole tracker.\n\n**Fix.** Configure one fetch refspec per namespace: `+refs/\u003cns\u003e/*:refs/remotes/\u003cremote\u003e/\u003cns\u003e/*`. Prune then maps each ref back to its real source and removes only refs that were actually deleted on the remote. Tested: go-git accepts these refspecs. A negative refspec (`^refs/heads/work-*`) also stops the pruning, but go-git rejects it (`malformed refspec, separators are wrong`) and git-work stops working, so that route is out.\n\n**Decision: an explicit `git work install`, not automatic (2026-11-05).** Tools routinely write their own namespaced config keys without asking (`lfs.repositoryformatversion`, `git-bug.identity`), but they don't silently change git's own settings like `remote.*.fetch`. That takes a dedicated git command (`git remote set-branches`) or an explicit setup step (`git lfs install`, `git annex init`). So pull doesn't edit the config; a command does. `install` rather than `init` (sounds like creating a tracker) or `configure` (sounds like a settings editor).\n\n**Spec.**\n- `git work install [REMOTE]`: REMOTE defaults to `git-work.remote`, falling back to `origin`, as pull and push do. For each tracker namespace, it adds `+refs/\u003cns\u003e/*:refs/remotes/\u003cREMOTE\u003e/\u003cns\u003e/*` to `remote.\u003cREMOTE\u003e.fetch` if that exact line is missing. It reads and writes via the git CLI (`gitcli`), only adds, never touches other lines, and running it again changes nothing. It prints nothing, per the CLI convention for writers. A remote that isn't configured is an error.\n- No Starlark binding: this is one-time checkout setup, the same exemption as `user new` and `user adopt` (`doc/design/cli-convention.md`). Record it there.\n- `git work pull` (and so `sync`): when any refspec is missing for the remote it pulls from, print one hint to stderr: `remote.\u003cR\u003e.fetch lacks the tracker's refspecs; git work install adds them`. It never edits the config. Skip the hint when the remote isn't a configured name (e.g. a URL).\n- Docs: in README and `host/quickstart.md`, the manual loop becomes \"run `git work install` once per clone\". Run `go generate`.\n\n**Also.** `entity/dag/entity.go:80` (`readRemote`) builds `refs/remotes/\u003cnamespace\u003e/\u003cremote\u003e/\u003cid\u003e`, with the namespace and remote swapped. Fix it, or delete it if it has no callers.","files":null}]}